\section{Parameter strings}{

The arguments that take further command-line parameters for an external program - \code{params}
(\code{\link{multi_aln}}, \code{\link{codon_aln}}), \code{aa_aln_params}, \code{aligner_params},
\code{diamond_params}, \code{blast_params}, \code{kaks_calc.params}, \code{deepclust_params},
\code{realign_params} - are \strong{lists of arguments, not shell text}. The string is split at white space into
arguments; single or double quotes keep an argument with spaces together
(\code{"--outfmt 6 --masking 'a b'"}), and a backslash takes the next character literally. Each argument is then
handed to the program as it is. Characters that mean something to a shell (\code{;}, \code{|}, \code{&},
\code{>}, \code{$( )}, backquotes, \code{*}) are not interpreted: \code{params = "--force; touch x"} gives the
program the three arguments \code{--force;}, \code{touch} and \code{x}, which it will reject. Redirections and
pipes inside such a string therefore do not work (they did, unintended, up to 0.4.3).
}

\section{Errors and warnings}{

The errors and warnings a caller may want to handle carry the class \code{"orthologr_error"} /
\code{"orthologr_warning"} and one or two classes for their kind, so that they can be caught without matching
the text of the message, e.g.
\code{tryCatch(dNdS(q, s), orthologr_error_tool_missing = function(e) message("please install it"))}:
\describe{
\item{\code{orthologr_error_tool_missing}}{an external program (DIAMOND, BLAST+, Perl, an alignment program,
KaKs_Calculator) was not found;}
\item{\code{orthologr_error_package_missing}}{an optional R package (Suggests) is needed but not installed (when
COAL is needed and neither \pkg{coalr} nor the coal-cpp program is there, also \code{orthologr_error_tool_missing});}
\item{\code{orthologr_error_tool_failed}}{an external program failed or wrote no output; with
\code{orthologr_error_alignment_failed} when it was an alignment program (PAL2NAL, Clustal, MAFFT, MUSCLE,
T-Coffee, COAL);}
\item{\code{orthologr_error_no_hits}}{the search found no (reciprocal best) hits, so there are no gene pairs;}
\item{\code{orthologr_error_bad_input}}{an input file cannot be used (it does not exist, it is empty, a protein file
given as coding sequences, duplicated sequence IDs);}
\item{\code{orthologr_error_bad_argument}}{an argument value is not supported;}
\item{\code{orthologr_warning_failed_pairs}}{some gene pairs could not be processed (their values are \code{NA},
the reasons are in \code{attr(result, "failed_pairs")});}
\item{\code{orthologr_warning_isoforms}}{an input file seems to hold several transcripts per gene;}
\item{\code{orthologr_warning_legacy_engine}}{the settings of 0.4.3 are in force
(\code{orthologr.legacy_pairwise_aln}, \code{\link{orthologr_legacy_options}}) but the COAL engine was asked for,
which cannot reproduce the numbers of 0.4.3.}
}
These classes are given by the checks of the arguments and input files of \code{\link{dNdS}},
\code{\link{dNdS_coal}}, \code{\link{dNdS_clusters}}, \code{\link{orthogroups}}, \code{\link{dNdS_orthogroups}}, \code{\link{divergence_stratigraphy}},
\code{\link{filter_dNdS}}, the alignment functions (\code{\link{pairwise_aln}}, \code{\link{multi_aln}},
\code{\link{codon_aln}}), \code{\link{substitutionrate}}, the \code{read.*()} functions and
\code{\link{read_dNdS}}, and by every check for a program or an optional package. Other, older functions of the
package may still signal plain errors (class \code{simpleError}).

A failure of one gene pair inside \code{dNdS()} is not an error: it becomes an \code{NA} row with its reason.
